speechtext:
1.	James White.
2.	57.
3.	$6,000.
4.	Mr. White's classification is the same as other deputy heads, namely "$4,200

and up," the "Rate to be determined by the Governor in Council."
5.	Mr. White has had a very wide experience in professional work extending over some 36 years.
When writing the Premier, Sir Wilfrid Laurier, under date October 27, 1909, the Chairman of the Commission, of Conservation requested the appointment of Mr. White as chief officer of the commission and added "I really do not know where we could look for another man who would be satisfactory."
1884 to 1894, Mr. White was engaged upon topographical and geological work on the staff of the Geological Survey of Canada;
1896-99, Geographer and Chief Draughtsman to the Geological Survey;
1899, appointed Chief Geographer of the Department of the Interior;
1901, compiled report on " Altitudes in Canada" which has saved many thousands of dollars during the railway construction;
1903, on Alaska Boundary Convention;
1906, * Completed Economic Atlas of Canada-the first comprehensive compilation of the resources of Canada ever undertaken;
1906,	elected full Member of the Canadian Society of Civil Engineers;
1907,	assisted in investigations respecting fast trans-Atlantic passenger steamships ("All-Red" Steamship Line);
From 1899 to 1909, Chief Geographer of the Department of the Interior and engaged in study of the resources of Canada and economic methods of the development of same;
1909, Mr. White was appointed Chief Officer of the Commission of Conservation;
1913, was given the rank of Deputy Minister.
6.	So far as known, no such increase has been proposed.
